WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Can you tell me what you do for work?

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.800
Currently, I'm a music teacher in Barcelona.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.300
I work in an international school,

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.800
teaching in English, and... yeah, primary and secondary school.

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.300
Okay, I used to be a teacher, but I retired in July.

6
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.800
I'm a postie, I work for Royal Mail.

7
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.300
So, I work part-time, doing that four days a week.

8
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.800
What do you do for a job?

9
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.300
So I work in a special education needs school in Lancing,

10
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.300
with children and young people with communication needs,

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.800
behavioural needs.

12
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.800
And we just... yeah, my role is pastoral and safeguarding,

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.300
so just trying to work with them, educate them...

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.300
Wow, okay. That's a mouthful.

15
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.300
Do you have a specific job title?

16
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.300
Yes, so I have five...

17
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.800
What do you do for work?

18
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:02.300
My name is Sjors and I work as a ramp employee at Schiphol Airport,

19
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.300
and I am a turnaround coordinator

20
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.300
and I coordinate the turnaround process of an aircraft.

21
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.300
I am an underwriter for an insurance company.

22
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.300
What do you do for a job?

23
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.300
So I work at a catering company,

24
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.300
where I basically just serve like food and drinks

25
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.300
at like weddings and corporate events.

26
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.800
Sometimes like, wakes and stuff, but it's mostly weddings.

27
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.800
I used to teach business studies and economics to kids from 11 to 19.

28
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.800
So, 'Pastoral Assistant', so the way that pastoral works in our school,

29
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.300
is basically, students that struggle to be in the classroom,

30
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.300
we'll deliver interventions to support them.

31
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.300
So it depends on the intervention really,

32
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.800
so it could be as little as an hour a week,

33
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.800
to help a student tie their shoelaces,

34
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.800
because they can't quite manage the classroom setting, yet.

35
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.800
But we do a lot of work to get them to that place,

36
00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.300
so that they can go into a classroom.

37
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.800
I just graduated from school.

38
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.300
I did hotel management, because in the future,

39
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.300
maybe, I want to have... to own a hotel,

40
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.300
so I thought, maybe this is a good start to begin with.

41
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.800
I did it for a year.

42
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.300
I did a fast-track, so I was graduating faster.

43
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.300
Can I ask; what do you do for work?

44
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.300
So a bit complicated actually, but I work for a university,

45
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.300
but I help other universities, in the country,

46
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.300
to do more work with their communities,

47
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.300
to be more, kind of place-based, or civic.

48
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.300
So it's kind of helping universities connect more deeply with...

49
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.800
with their people, and their places and their communities.

50
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.300
Go on, you first. A healthcare assistant

51
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.300
and work with elderly with dementia.

52
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:48.800
Nice, is that around here? No, no, Kent. In Kent? Yeah. Okay.

53
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:56.800
And I'm a tattoo artist.

54
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.300
How did both of you fall into that line of work?

55
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.800
Was it something you wanted to do?

56
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.300
Did it just happen? Gosh.

57
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.800
I've always wanted to be a tattoo artist, really, since I was a kid.

58
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.800
Just drawing non-stop, just drawing cartoons, just loving it.

59
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.300
And then I thought, if I could make a career out of it, why not?

60
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.300
And for me, I had a huge career change.

61
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.300
My first job was actually in the police,

62
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.800
in the Metropolitan Police. Oh, really!? Yeah.

63
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.300
And then, I just wanted to give a little bit more back to,

64
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.800
I don't know... the community... yeah,

65
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.300
Just like helping people.

66
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.300
That's great. And then, how long have you been a postie for?

67
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:44.300
I've been doing it for like, 10 years now, for my sins.

68
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.300
How did you get into that line of work?

69
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.800
I just wanted something part-time,

70
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.800
because I was trying to do art, and design, and music

71
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.800
and I didn't really know where I was going with all that,

72
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.300
so I wanted something a little bit stable,

73
00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.300
to just keep me going afloat, while I was working towards all that.

74
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.300
And yeah, it was meant to be a stopgap,

75
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.300
but I quite enjoy it and it's kept me going, so yeah.

76
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.300
Where does the passion come from,

77
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.800
for you to want to own your own hotel?

78
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:14.300
Or where did it come from to work, or be in sort of, hospitality?

79
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.800
Where did this passion come from?

80
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:20.300
I really like to work with people, but also help people.

81
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.300
I really like to give some advice to people,

82
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.300
or chat with some people and just make people have a good time.

83
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.300
Okay, and is catering, is hospitality your kind of...

84
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.800
something you wanted to get into initially,

85
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.800
or do you have another idea?

86
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.800
It's sort of a good way to make money while I'm studying,

87
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.300
but I don't think it's something that I necessarily,

88
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.800
want to do long term.

89
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.800
It can be quite like, intense.

90
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.300
I work like, quite long hours and stuff.

91
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.800
So it's like, good money while you're a student,

92
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.800
and you're young and you have energy.

93
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.300
But, I don't think it's a forever thing for me.

94
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.300
And, did you train to do this?

95
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.800
Did you want to do this? No.

96
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.300
No, I studied over there, at Brighton Uni, actually.

97
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:06.800
I did history in politics,

98
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.800
and my aim was to get into government charity work.

99
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:12.800
But, there wasn't really many jobs going when I graduated,

100
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.300
so I got a job as an insurance broker, and then 12 years later,

101
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.300
I'm working in underwriting for a global insurer, so...

102
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.800
And you enjoy it? Yeah, it's so much fun.

103
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.300
Yeah, really? It's great stuff.

104
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.300
Really busy, but it's interesting.

105
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.300
And what would you say is the best thing about your job?

106
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.300
Creativity. Because I'm very much autonomous,

107
00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.300
I can do um... left to my own devices,

108
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.300
so I can kind of do anything I like with any class. That's great

109
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.800
So yeah, the creativity and the...

110
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.300
I think being a teacher is very social

111
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.300
and I don't really think you can be ultimately that sad

112
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.800
and isolated being a teacher, because you're very communicative,

113
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.800
you're always... children are hilarious

114
00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:53.300
and they're always talking to you

115
00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.800
and asking you, how you're doing and you know,

116
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.040
asking funny questions and so, it keeps on your toes.
